    <h2 class="section-heading">Background</h2>

    <div class="prose-column space-y-6 text-[var(--color-text-secondary)] leading-relaxed text-base md:text-lg">
      <p>
        I am an incoming Master's student in Computer Science at Syracuse University, starting Fall 2026, alongside several years of experience running help desk operations and supervising student technology consultants at Syracuse University ITS. My academic focus is formal verification applied to modern ML systems infrastructure, an area where formal methods have barely been applied. I am currently looking for a thesis advisor and will update this once one is confirmed.
      </p>

      <p>
        The throughline across my work is treating a formal specification as something that produces insight, not just documentation. Writing a precise spec of a distributed training protocol forces explicit questions (what is genuinely atomic, which synchronization barriers are actually required, what correctness really demands) and those questions tend to reveal optimizations.
      </p>

      <p>
        This research builds directly on my formal-methods coursework. <span class="tech-tag">Temporal logic</span> and the <span class="tech-tag">mu-calculus</span>, the logics I studied for reasoning about concurrent systems, express exactly the <span class="tech-tag">safety & liveness</span> properties my <span class="tech-tag">TLA+</span> specifications target, so the thesis is a continuation of that foundation rather than a departure from it.
      </p>
    </div>

